  "stem": "Relative to an origin $O$, the points $P$, $Q$ and $R$ have position vectors $\\mathbf{a} + 2\\mathbf{b}$, $3\\mathbf{a} + 3\\mathbf{b}$ and $7\\mathbf{a} + 5\\mathbf{b}$, where $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$ are not parallel. Show that $P$, $Q$ and $R$ are collinear, and state the ratio $PQ : QR$.",
  "answerSpec": {
   "markScheme": {
    "points": [
     {
      "id": "p1",
      "description": "M1 finds $\\overrightarrow{PQ} = 2\\mathbf{a} + \\mathbf{b}$.",
      "marks": 1
     },
     {
      "id": "p2",
      "description": "M1 finds $\\overrightarrow{QR} = 4\\mathbf{a} + 2\\mathbf{b}$.",
      "marks": 1
     },
     {
      "id": "p3",
      "description": "A1 states $\\overrightarrow{QR} = 2\\overrightarrow{PQ}$, so the lines are parallel, and they share the point $Q$, so $P$, $Q$, $R$ are collinear.",
      "marks": 1
     },
     {
      "id": "p4",
      "description": "B1 states $PQ : QR = 1 : 2$.",
      "marks": 1
     }
    ]
   },
   "modelAnswer": "$$\n\\overrightarrow{PQ} = (3\\mathbf{a} + 3\\mathbf{b}) - (\\mathbf{a} + 2\\mathbf{b}) = 2\\mathbf{a} + \\mathbf{b}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\overrightarrow{QR} = (7\\mathbf{a} + 5\\mathbf{b}) - (3\\mathbf{a} + 3\\mathbf{b}) = 4\\mathbf{a} + 2\\mathbf{b} = 2(2\\mathbf{a} + \\mathbf{b}) = 2\\overrightarrow{PQ}\n$$\n\nSo $PQ$ is parallel to $QR$; since $Q$ is a common point, $P$, $Q$ and $R$ are collinear, with $PQ : QR = 1 : 2$."
  },
  "markingMode": "MANUAL",
  "explanation": "Show one vector is a multiple of the other, then mention the common point.",
  "hints": [
   "End minus start for each vector.",
   "Do not forget the common point."
  ],
  "skill": "ANALYSIS",
  "calculator": false
 },
 {
  "key": "vectors.geometry.q15",
  "type": "SHORT_ANSWER",
  "concepts": [
   "amath.vectors.geometry"
  ],
  "difficulty": 3,
  "marks": 2,
  "estimatedSeconds": 180,
  "stem": "Explain why, when $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$ are non-zero and not parallel, the equation $p\\mathbf{a} + q\\mathbf{b} = 3\\mathbf{a} - 2\\mathbf{b}$ means that $p = 3$ and $q = -2$.",
  "answerSpec": {
   "markScheme": {
    "points": [
     {
      "id": "p1",
      "description": "Rearranges to $(p - 3)\\mathbf{a} = (-2 - q)\\mathbf{b}$.",
      "marks": 1
     },
     {
      "id": "p2",
      "description": "Explains that a multiple of $\\mathbf{a}$ can equal a multiple of $\\mathbf{b}$ only if both multiples are zero (they are not parallel), so $p = 3$ and $q = -2$.",
      "marks": 1
     }
    ]
   },
   "markingNotes": "Example answer: Rearranging gives $(p - 3)\\mathbf{a} = (-2 - q)\\mathbf{b}$. If $p - 3 \\neq 0$, this would make $\\mathbf{a}$ a multiple of $\\mathbf{b}$, i.e. parallel to it, which is false. So $p - 3 = 0$ and then $(-2 - q)\\mathbf{b} = \\mathbf{0}$, so $q = -2$."
  },
  "markingMode": "MANUAL",
  "explanation": "Non-parallel vectors are independent directions: one cannot be built from the other.",
  "hints": [
   "Collect the a terms on one side and the b terms on the other."
  ],
  "skill": "UNDERSTANDING",
  "calculator": false
 },
